A||||5||fierospeeder||spray paint||||11
Z||000000||fierospeeder||10-20-1999||11:42 PM||||I had a question, i see all fieros have the common problem of paint fading.  I was wondering if i can spray paint the roof, hood and trunk.  The sides are perfect.  Can i sand the hood, degrease it, and paint it with auto spray paint then after a couple of coats spray on some clear coat.  Just wondering if anyone has done this and you got any tips.  I will be buying a paintgun later to do my beetle project but just want my car to look hotter so i can get more speeding tickets.<BR>thanks||||reg||11
Z||000001||mwbackus||10-21-1999||02:25 AM||||     I have done a little of this, but only on "very" small areas that can be feathered in easily.  The side mirrors can removed and sprayed with fairly good results.  It is quite hard to prevent larger droplets of paint messing up the finish, so I would never use more than about 1/2 of a can before switching to a new one, and wipe the nozzle frequently.  <BR>     It took quite a bit of practice before I could perfect the technique needed to get anything near good results from the can. (ie.. the angle and distance from the can to the surface needs to be controlled precisly).  <BR>     I would certainly not expect the finish of the "can method" to be anything near as durable as a that achieved with a gun and a good quality base/clear coat paint.  <P>     For large areas like you describe, it would probably be better to just remove the part and have it done professionally.  The roof may be a consideration, since it is much harder to remove, but be aware that matching the paint to a weathered surface is nearly impossible using spray can paint.  <P>Mike<P>||||reg||1
Z||000002||deceler8||10-21-1999||10:02 AM||||Tried the spray paint method and it worked well. I only paid $500 for my '86 2m4, which had a faded roof and all paint burned off the mirrors and luggage rack. Painted the black items off the car with black Rustoleum spray and the roof with spray can mixed by my local auto parts store. I masked off everything but the roof panel, fixed the chips, sanded it down with 400, 800 and 1500 grit and the sprayed 3 or 4 coats, followed by clear out of a spray can. <P>Didn't look great initially, but after drying a week, I went over it with some 1000, 1500, and 2000 grit wet paper, and then compound, and it looks like the rest of the car.<P>Damn best looking $500 car I've ever seen. Just repainting the mirrors and rack made a world of difference.||||reg||10
Z||000003||deceler8||10-21-1999||10:11 AM||||Also forgot...I painted the rear decklid vents with black gloss engine enamel (high temp) as the paint was also shot on them. Just roughed them up with 240 grit sandpaper and cleaned them with solvent. They look great too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lowCG||10-21-1999||10:20 AM||||Backus is right about the droplets,and the durability(you'll have to wax the car every two weeks or so to keep the shine).Takes at least two coats to get good flow out,sanding very lightly one day after the first coat.<BR>I have a black faded car(roof,hood)and am trying to think of possible solutions,not sure I want to hassle with the cans again,am too lazy to want to wax my car every week.<BR>Whatever you do,make sure and get rid of all of the oxidised paint that's left on the car now.||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||10-21-1999||10:31 AM||||Mainly went the spray route as it's getting cold here on the Northern Plains and didn't want to look at the crappy roof and exterior pieces all winter.<P>Spray cans obviously aren't as good as a real paint job, but they bought me time until spring, when I can properly paint the car.||||reg||1
